\section{Systematization of Remedies}
\label{sec:remedies}

We now organize the defenses the community has developed and map each to the stages it protects.
Table~\ref{tab:remedies} summarizes fourteen remedy families, the stages they cover, whether they
prevent, detect, or contain a failure, their residual risk, and the friction that governs their
deployment. We then isolate the assurance blind spot that the coverage matrix makes visible.

\begin{table}[tp]
\centering
\caption{Fourteen remedy families mapped to the pipeline. Mode: \prevent{} prevents the failure,
\detect{} detects it, \contain{} limits its blast radius. Friction is the practical barrier to
adoption: Lo, a library or platform default exists; Med, it needs configuration or platform
support; Hi, it needs new tooling, hardware, or user action. No family covers every stage.}
\label{tab:remedies}
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.12}
\begin{tabular}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{3.9cm} l c >{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{4.2cm} c@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Remedy family} & \textbf{Stages} & \textbf{Mode} & \textbf{Residual risk} & \textbf{Friction} \\
\midrule
R1 Vetted entropy source $+$ health tests~\cite{nist80090b} & \stage{1},\stage{2} & \prevent\,\detect & Opaque hardware source may still be weak or subverted (T4) & Med \\
R2 Standardized CSPRNG/DRBG~\cite{nist80090a,kelsey1999yarrow} & \stage{3} & \prevent & Side-channel state recovery if misused~\cite{cohney2020blackswan} & Lo \\
R3 Robust seeding, block until seeded~\cite{dodis2013devrandom,corrigangibbs2013ensuring} & \stage{1},\stage{4} & \prevent & Boot-time stalls tempt unsafe non-blocking calls & Med \\
R4 Continuous reseed $+$ fork/VM hygiene~\cite{everspaugh2014whirlwind,ristenpart2010vmreset} & \stage{5} & \prevent\,\contain & Requires host cooperation (virtio-rng, fork hooks) & Med \\
R5 Hedged nonces (RFC 6979 $+$ noise)~\cite{rfc6979,bernstein2012ed25519,cfrg2025hedged} & \stage{6} & \prevent & Pure determinism aids fault/side-channel attacks & Lo \\
R6 Hedged/derandomized crypto~\cite{bellare2009hedged} & \stage{4}--\stage{6} & \prevent\,\contain & Not yet default in most libraries and protocols & Med \\
R7 Threshold keygen, public randomness beacons & \stage{4},\stage{6} & \prevent\,\contain & Coordination cost; wrong trust model for local key custody & Hi \\
R8 Statistical tests $+$ certification~\cite{nist80090b} & \stage{1}--\stage{3} & \detect & \textbf{Blind to uniform-but-predictable and uniform-but-chosen output} & Med \\
R9 Reproducible builds, RNG boot self-test, attestation~\cite{coinkite2026backgrounder} & \stage{$\ast$} & \prevent\,\detect & Immature tooling; self-test must exercise the real path & Hi \\
R10 User entropy $+$ passphrase (dice, BIP-39)~\cite{bip39} & \stage{1},\stage{4} & \prevent\,\contain & Depends on user diligence; poor default & Hi \\
R11 Formal verification of RNG code & \stage{2},\stage{3},\stage{5} & \prevent & Specifications may omit the deployed threat (\stage{$\ast$}) & Hi \\
R12 Tamper/environmental sensors, fail-closed~\cite{ledger2026coldcard} & \stage{1},\stage{2} & \prevent\,\detect & Protects the source, not the wiring that selects it & Med \\
R13 Verifiable entropy protocols (commit-reveal)~\cite{trezor2025entropycheck} & \stage{1},\stage{4},\stage{$\ast$} & \prevent\,\detect & Needs a host verifier; covers keygen, not later use & Med \\
R14 Anti-exfil / sign-to-contract nonces~\cite{bitbox2019antiklepto,blockstream2020antiexfil} & \stage{6} & \prevent & Needs host support; per-signature protocol cost & Med \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


Appendix~\ref{app:remedystage} discusses the families stage by stage. Here we concentrate on the
finding that the coverage matrix makes unavoidable.

\subsection{The assurance blind spot}
\label{sec:blindspot}
That statistical testing cannot establish unpredictability is well
known~\cite{aumasson2024serious,nist80090b}: the hash of a counter passes every battery. The
corpus shows how this limit plays out in deployment. Batteries such as the NIST suite, Dieharder,
and TestU01, and the certification regimes built on them, judge a generator by its output
distribution. The continuous health tests that SP 800-90B requires, the repetition count test
and the adaptive proportion test, catch a source that is stuck or drifting~\cite{nist80090b}, and
a check on the returned values would flag the zeros of AMD's \texttt{RDSEED}. These tests run on
the noise source, however, so they certify that the source is alive, not that key generation
consulted it. In the coverage matrix (Table~\ref{tab:coverage}), R8 prevents none of the
twenty-eight incidents and detects only the two that are statistically visible, MIFARE and
\texttt{RDSEED}. It is blind to output that is uniform but predictable, as in \dualec{}, Milk Sad,
and \coldcard{}, and to output that is uniform but adversarially chosen
(Appendix~\ref{sec:covert})~\cite{bernstein2016dualec,checkoway2014dualec,milksad2023,coinkite2026backgrounder}.
Unpredictability is a property of the input distribution and of the wiring, so it has to be
established structurally: by removing randomness from the security argument where the primitive
allows (R5, R6), by verifying that the deployed path reaches the intended source (R9), and by
vetting the source itself (R1).

\subsection{A prioritized checklist for implementers}
\label{sec:checklist}
We order the items by the coverage matrix of Section~\ref{sec:effectiveness}: first the family
that prevents the build and integration failures, which no other family reaches, then the
families that prevent the most corpus incidents, and last those that only detect or contain.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=1pt,topsep=2pt]
\item \textbf{Bind the build to the intended source, and the binary to reviewed source.} Make the
absence of the hardware generator a compile-time error rather than a runtime fallback, and publish
reproducible builds. These measures form R9, the only family that prevents the three
\stage{$\ast$} failures in the
corpus~\cite{yilek2009debian,checkoway2016juniper,coinkite2026backgrounder}.
\item \textbf{Test the real path at power-on.} Run a known-answer test that exercises the
deployed entropy path and halts the device on failure.
\item \textbf{Use the platform's vetted CSPRNG, never a general-purpose PRNG.} R2 prevents nine
corpus incidents, from MIFARE to Ill Bloom~\cite{nist80090a}.
\item \textbf{Block until seeded, and reseed on fork, snapshot, and resume.} R3 and R4 prevent
eight and three corpus incidents~\cite{dodis2013devrandom,everspaugh2014whirlwind}.
\item \textbf{Use hedged nonces.} Derive signature nonces deterministically and fold in fresh
secret noise~\cite{rfc6979,cfrg2025hedged}. R5 prevents four corpus incidents, but not the timing
leaks of Minerva and TPM-Fail, which need constant-time code.
\item \textbf{Hedge what remains.} Derive key material from the secret, the input, and the
available randomness together, so that security survives one weak input~\cite{bellare2009hedged}.
\item \textbf{Adopt anti-exfil signing.} Where a host is present, use sign-to-contract so the
device cannot choose the nonce alone~\cite{bitbox2019antiklepto,blockstream2020antiexfil}.
\item \textbf{Verify that the combiner consumes every source.} Mixing helps only if each input
reaches the state, so audit the width of the reseed path~\cite{block2026coldcard}.
\item \textbf{Let the user add entropy.} Dice or an equivalent input, plus a passphrase, survives
a total generator failure~\cite{bip39,coldcard2026dice}.
\item \textbf{Offer a proof of freshness.} Where a host is present at key generation, use a
commit-reveal entropy check~\cite{trezor2025entropycheck}.
\item \textbf{Harden the source physically} where the threat model includes physical
access~\cite{ledger2026coldcard,martin2015strng}.
\item \textbf{Plan for migration.} A patch cannot repair an existing key, so prepare the tooling
and instructions for moving to new keys before they are needed.
\end{enumerate}
